"Material Resources, Productivity and the Environment (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2015/02/material-resources-productivity-and-the-environment_g1g27386/9789264190504-en.pdf)\nciteturn270view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 176; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn267view0\",\"lineno\":2310}); Total lines: 8094\nL2287@P60: 4 For instance, the green growth strategy of Chile; the circular economy roadmap in France; and the Green\nL2288@P60: Economy Action Plan in Switzerland.\nL2289@P60: 5 For instance, the United States Sustainable Manufacturing Initiative (SMI) and Public-Private Dialogue\nL2290@P60: launched by the Department of Commerce to support United States companies in their sustainable\nL2291@P60: manufacturing efforts; the Material-efficiency Centre in Finland; or the Resource efficiency network set up\nL2292@P60: by the German Ministry of the Environment.\nL2293@P60: REFERENCES\nL2294@P60: • European Commission, Resource Efficiency,\nL2295@P60: http://ec.europa.eu/environment/resource_efficiency/about/index_en.htm\nL2296@P60: • European Commission, Sustainable use of natural resources, http://ec.europa.eu/environment/natres/index.htm\nL2297@P60: • EEA (European Environment Agency ) (2010), The European Environment: State and Outlook 2010 –Material\nL2298@P60: Resources and Waste, www.eea.europa.eu/soer/europe/material-resources-and-waste.\nL2299@P60: • OECD (2014), Green Growth Indicators 2014, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL2300@P60: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264202030-en.\nL2301@P60: • OECD (2011) Resource Productivity in the G8 and OECD: A Report in the Framework of the Kobe 3R Action Plan,\nL2302@P60: www.oecd.org/document/14/0,3746,en_2649_34395_47926478_1_1_1_1,00.html.\nL2303@P60: • OECD (2011), Towards Green Growth: Monitoring Progress: OECD Indicators, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD\nL2304@P60: Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264111356-en.\nL2305@P60: • OECD (2008), Recommendation of the Council on Resource Productivity [C(2008)40], OECD, Paris, available at\nL2306@P60: available at http://webnet.oecd.org/oecdacts/.\nL2307@P60: • OECD (2008b), Measuring Material Flows and Resource Productivity.\nL2308@P60: www.oecd.org/environment/resourceefficiency.\nL2309@P60: • OECD (2008c), OECD Environmental Outlook to 2030, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL2310@P60: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264040519-en.\nL2311@P60: • OECD (2004), Recommendation of the Council on Material Flows and Resource Productivity [C(2004)79], Paris,\nL2312@P60: available at http://webnet.oecd.org/oecdacts/.\nL2313@P60: • OECD project on measuring economic performance and social progress, www.oecd.org/statistics/measuring\u0002economic-social-progress/aboutthehigh-levelexpertgroup.htm\nL2314@P60: • OECD work on Green Growth, www.oecd.org/greengrowth/gg-sd-2012.htm.\nL2315@P60: • SERI/WU (Sustainable Europe Resource Institute/Vienna University) (2014), Global Material Flow Database. 2014\nL2316@P60: Version. Available at www.materialflows.net.\nL2317@P60: • UN (2014), System of Environmental-Economic Accounting 2012: Central framework, European Commission, FAO,\nL2318@P60: IMF, OECD, UN, the World Bank, United Nations, New York.\nL2319@P60: http://unstats.un.org/unsd/envaccounting/seeaRev/SEEA_CF_Final_en.pdf.\nL2320@P60: • UNEP (United Nations Environment Programme ) (2013), International Resource Panel,\nL2321@P60-62: www.unep.org/resourcepanel. CHAPTER 4. THE MATERIAL BASIS OF THE GLOBAL ECONOMY\nL2322@P62: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 61\nL2323@P62: Chapter 4. The material basis of the global\nL2324@P62: economy\nL2325@P62: This chapter examines global trends in material flows and resource\nL2326@P62: productivity using tools from Material Flow Analysis (MFA) and data from Material\nL2327@P62: Flow Accounting (see the Reader’s Guide for data sources). In MFA, the term\nL2328@P62: \"materials\" is often used in a broad sense, so as to encompass all material-related\nL2329@P62: flows arising at all stages of the material cycle. It refers to both materials and\nL2330@P62: products derived from natural resources that are used as inputs into human\nL2331@P62: activities, as well as residuals (such as waste or pollutant emissions) arising from\nL2332@P62: their extraction and use, and ecosystem inputs (such as nutrients, carbon dioxide,\nL2333@P62: and oxygen) required for their extraction and use. Here the focus is on “material\nL2334@P62: resources” that designate the usable materials or substances (raw materials, energy)\nL2335@P62: produced from natural resources. These usable \"materials\" include energy carriers\nL2336@P62: (gas, oil, coal), metal ores and metals, construction minerals and other minerals,\nL2337@P62-63: soil and biomass. Ecosystem inputs and pollutant outputs are not considered. CHAPTER 4. THE MATERIAL BASIS OF THE GLOBAL ECONOMY\nL2338@P63: 62 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL2339@P63: MATERIAL EXTRACTION RATES\nL2340@P63: Global extraction of material resources continues to grow\nL2341@P63: The amount of materials extracted, harvested and consumed worldwide reached nearly 72 billion\nL2342@P63: metric tonnes (Gt) per year in 2010. This is twice as much as in 1980 when global material\nL2343@P63: extraction was around 36 Gt, and an estimated ten-fold increase since the early 1900s when\nL2344@P63: extraction was estimated at around 7 Gt (SERI/WU material flows database; Krausman et al.,\nL2345@P63: 2009). Based on these global figures, OECD countries accounted for 27% of domestic extraction of\nL2346@P63: used materials (DEU) worldwide in 2010 (compared to 46% in 1980), while the BRIICS countries\nL2347@P63: (Brazil, Russia, India, Indonesia, China and South Africa) accounted for 51% (compared to 30% in\nL2348@P63: 1980).1\nL2349@P63:  Although more updated global figures are not currently available, given the limited growth\nL2350@P63: of the global economy since the 2008 financial crisis, material use likely stays at around 72-75 Gt\nL2351@P63: today.\nL2352@P63: Material use is expected to continue growing, in line with economic activity, with one projection\nL2353@P63: expecting it to reach 100 Gt by 2030.2\nL2354@P63: Figure 4.1. Global material resource extraction, trends and materials mix, 1980-2010\nL2355@P63: Source: SERI/WU (2014) material flows database, www.materialflows.net.\nL2356@P63: Domestic extraction used (DEU) measures the flow of materials that originate from the environment and physically enter the\nL2357@P63: economy to be transformed into or incorporated in products. These materials are usually of economic value.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaterial Resources, Productivity and the Environment (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2015/02/material-resources-productivity-and-the-environment_g1g27386/9789264190504-en.pdf)\nciteturn270view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 176; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn267view0\",\"lineno\":4810}); Total lines: 8094\nL4788@P106: Indicator Analysis, IEA, Paris.\nL4789@P106: • INECE (International Network for Environmental Compliance and Enforcement) (2010), International Hazardous\nL4790@P106: Waste Inspection Project at Seaports: Results and Recommendations, INECE Seaport Environmental Security\nL4791@P106: Network, INECE, Washington, D.C.\nL4792@P106: • Joardar, S.D. (2000), “Urban Residential Solid Waste Management in India: Issues related to Institutional\nL4793@P106: Arrangements,” Public Works Management & Policy, Vol. 4, No. 4, p. 319-330.\nL4794@P106: • Krausmann, F. et al. (2009), “Growth in global material use, GDP and population during the 20th century,” Ecological\nL4795@P106: Economics, Vol. 68, pages 2696-2705.\nL4796@P106: • Kapur, A. and Graedel, T.E