 sales eclipse 2.5M mark, now climbing at 2,000 per day”, AutoblogGreen, 2 July\nL7741@P166: 2010, green.autoblog.com/2010/07/02/toyotas-hybrid-sales-eclipse-2-5m-mark-now-climbing-at-2-000-p/\nL7742@P166: • Lusty, Paul (2010), “Critical Metals and Rare Earth Elements,” Mines and Money 2010, British Geological Survey,\nL7743@P166: Keyworth, Nottingham.\nL7744@P166: • Margonelli, Lisa (2009), “Clean Energy’s Dirty Little Secret,” The Atlantic Monthly, May 2009.\nL7745@P166: • Molycorp Minerals (2010), “Rare Earth Minerals: The Indispensable Resource for Clean Energy Technologies”,\nL7746@P166: presentation by Mark A. Smith to the Colorado Mining Association, 10 February 2010.\nL7747@P166: • Montgomery, Michael (2011), “Rare Earth Elements Monthly Recap: July,” Rare Earth Investing News, 1 August 2011,\nL7748@P166: http://rareearthinvestingnews.com/4463/rare-earth-elements-monthly-recap-july/\nL7749@P166: • OECD (2012a), Case Study on Critical Metals in Mobile Phones: Final Report,\nL7750@P166: http://www.oecd.org/env/waste/Case%20Study%20on%20Critical%20Metals%20in%20Mobile%20Phones.pdf.\nL7751@P166: • Schüler, D. et al. (2011), “A Study on Rare Earth and Their Recycling,” Final Report for the Greens/EFA Group in the\nL7752@P166: European Parliament, Öko-Institut e.V., Freiburg.\nL7753@P166: • Seaman, John (2010), Rare Earths and Clean Energy: Analyzing China’s Upper Hand, Institut français des relations\nL7754@P166: internationales, Paris.\nL7755@P166: • U.S. Department of Energy (2010), Critical Materials Strategy, U.S. Department of Energy, Washington, D.C.\nL7756@P166: • USGS (2011a), Mineral Commodity Summaries 2011, USGS, Reston, Virginia.\nL7757@P166: • USGS (2011b), written communication, 3 October 2011.\nL7758@P166: • USGS (2010a), Mineral Commodity Summaries 2010, USGS, Reston, Virginia.\nL7759@P166: • USGS (2010b), “Rare earths statistics,” in Kelly, T.D., and Matos, G.R., comps., Historical statistics for mineral and\nL7760@P166: material commodities in the United States, USGS Data Series 140 [Accessed 28 September 2010].\nL7761@P166: • USGS (2010c), 2008 Minerals Yearbook: Rare Earths [Advance Release], U.S. Geological Survey, Reston, Virginia\nL7762@P166: • USGS (2010d), The Principal Rare Earth Elements Deposits of the United States—A Summary of Domestic Deposits\nL7763@P166-167: and a Global Perspective, Scientific Investigations Report 2010–5220, USGS, Reston, Virginia. GLOSSARY\nL7764@P167: 166 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL7765@P167: GLOSSARY\nL7766@P167: Decoupling\nL7767@P167: The term decoupling refers to breaking the link between “environmental bads” and “economic goods.” Using this\nL7768@P167: concept helps analyse and assess the linkages between environmental degradation and economic development.\nL7769@P167: In practice, the measurement of decoupling refers to the relative growth rates of a direct pressure on the\nL7770@P167: environment and of an economically relevant variable to which it is causally linked. Decoupling occurs when the\nL7771@P167: growth rate of the environmental pressure (EP) is less than that of its economic driving force (DF) over a given\nL7772@P167: period. One distinguishes between absolute and relative decoupling. Decoupling is said to be absolute when the\nL7773@P167: environmental variable is stable or decreasing while the economic variable is growing. Decoupling is said to be\nL7774@P167: relative when environmental variable is increasing, but at a lower rate than the economic variable.\nL7775@P167: The decoupling concept has no automatic link to the environment’s capacity to sustain, absorb or resist\nL7776@P167: pressures of various kinds (deposition, discharges, harvests). A meaningful interpretation of the relationship of\nL7777@P167: EP to economic DF will require additional information. Also, the relationship between economic DF and EP,\nL7778@P167: more often than not, is complex. Most DF have multiple environmental effects, and most EP are generated by\nL7779@P167: multiple DF, which, in turn, are affected by societal responses. Changes in decoupling may thus be decomposed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaterial Resources, Productivity and the Environment (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2015/02/material-resources-productivity-and-the-environment_g1g27386/9789264190504-en.pdf)\nciteturn270view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 176; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn267view0\",\"lineno\":7365}); Total lines: 8094\nL7341@P157: Feed the World 2050, High-Level Expert Forum, Rome, 12-15 October 2009, FAO, Rome.\nL7342@P157: • FAO (2004), Use of Phosphate Rocks for Sustainable Agriculture, FAO, Rome.\nL7343@P157: • Heffer, S. and Prud’homme, M. (2009), “Fertilizer Outlook 2009-2013,” 77th International Fertiliser Association\nL7344@P157: Annual Conference, 25-27 May 2009, Shanghai, People’s Republic of China, IFA, Paris.\nL7345@P157: • IEA (International Energy Agency) (2009), Energy Technology Perspectives 2010: Scenarios and Strategies to 2050,\nL7346@P157: OECD Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/energy_tech-2010-en.\nL7347@P157: • IFA (International Fertilizer Industry Association) (2010), Production and trade statistics website,\nL7348@P157: www.fertilizer.org/ifa/Home-Page/STATISTICS/Production-and-trade [Accessed September 2010].\nL7349@P157: • Liu, Y., et al. (2008), “Global phosphorus flows and environmental impacts from a consumption perspective,” Journal\nL7350@P157: of Industrial Ecology, 12(4), p. 229-247.\nL7351@P157: • Maene, L.M. (2000), “Efficient Fertilizer Use and its Role in Increasing Food Production and Protecting the\nL7352@P157: Environment,” 6th AFA International Annual Conference, 31 January to 3 February 2000, Cairo.\nL7353@P157: • Morrison, L. (2009), “Enough Phosphorus for the Future” Corn and Soybean Digest online, February 15,