 FACTSHEET\nL7396@P159: From relatively obscurity rare earth elements have grown to become essential components in\nL7397@P159: a wide range of high tech, alternative energy and military applications. Although crustally\nL7398@P159: abundant, concentrations that can be economically extracted are rare. China’s dominance over\nL7399@P159: global supplies in the face of rising global demand are raising fears of shortages that risk disrupting\nL7400@P159: economies and derailing green growth plans. As with other metals, refining rare earths is energy\u0002and water-intensive, with added complexity due to their similar chemical properties. While there is\nL7401@P159: renewed interest in 3R and circular economy approaches, the recycling and reuse of rare earths\nL7402@P159: remains uneconomic and remains near zero.\nL7403@P159: See the Reader’s guide for information about the selection of key material resources.\nL7404@P159: Overview\nL7405@P159: Characteristics and uses\nL7406@P159: Rare earths are not a single element, but a group of 17 chemically similar metallic elements found\nL7407@P159: in the lanthanides series in the periodic table. Yttrium and Scandium are considered rare earth\nL7408@P159: elements (REE); they are often recovered with REE and share many of the same chemical\n"
